    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nMercedes Stops Individual Orders for the EQE Sedan, EQE SUV, and EQS SUV in Germany\n10 photos\nPhoto: Mercedes-Benz\nIf you live in Germany and intend to configure an EQE Sedan today, I'm afraid you are too late. Mercedes-Benz Deutschland has quietly revised its online configurator, deleting the full-electric sedan and two EQS-branded sport utility vehicles in the process. The configurator informs prospective customers that said models are \"no longer available for individual order,\" directing EQE Sedan, EQE SUV, and EQS SUV buyers to vehicles that are currently in stock.\nCuriously enough, the EQS Sedan gets to stay. The German automaker from Stuttgart also revised it substantially for model year 2027, with updates including a steer-by-wire system and 800-volt goodies for a driving range of up to 926 kilometers (575 miles) in the WLTP for the rear-drive 450+ version.\nFunny thing, Merc's ordering restrictions in the German market extend to a few additional European markets, including the United Kingdom, France, and the Netherlands. Even so, the Stuttgart-based manufacturer has yet to officially explain what is actually happening to these three models on the Old Continent.\nStopping individual orders is a different thing from ending series production, but looking at the bigger picture, Mercedes is preparing to replace them all. The E-Class with EQ Technology comes to mind, a mid-size electric sedan that will soon replace the EQE Sedan in the company's passenger vehicle lineup.\nBoth sedans and both sport utility vehicles are based on the Electric Vehicle Architecture, which is the automaker's first dedicated all-electric platform. Going forward, the passenger vehicle division will rely on two main platforms: MMA for compacts and MB.EA for midsizers. Then there's the AMG.EA of the GT 4-Door Coupe and its SUV-bodied sibling, whereas the van division flaunts the VAN.EA architecture for the luxury-oriented VLE and VLS.\nPhoto: Mercedes-AMG Mercedes launched the EVA-based EQS Sedan in 2021, followed by the EQE twins a year later. Together with the EQS SUV, they represented the company's failed attempt to establish a dedicated luxury-oriented electric vehicle family with styling distinct from their internal combustion counterparts.\nI remember YouTuber Arun Maini, a.k.a. mrwhosetheboss, buying an EQS Sedan for his mom a few years ago. Unfortunately for Mercedes, not even this kind of exposure helped the EQS Sedan from a commercial standpoint. All four models fell flat on their faces, selling very few units in pretty much every market where Mercedes is present.\nIt got so bad in the United States of America that Mercedes pulled the plug on the EQE Sedan and EQE SUV there after only four years on the market. In any case, the EQS Sedan soldiers on with very desirable updates and figures, including a 13 percent driving range improvement over the pre-facelift.\nIts 800-volt electrical system handles up to 350 kilowatts, allowing the full-size electric luxury sedan to recover 320 kilometers (199 miles) of WLTP driving range in 10 minutes. A larger battery is on the menu as well, along with new electric drive units developed from scratch rather than based on the previous drive units.\nMercedes also took inspiration from the Volkswagen Group with a two-speed transmission for the rear-mounted electric drive unit, allowing it to prioritize acceleration performance in first and efficiency in second. Speaking of taking inspiration, Mercedes also rolled out a yoke-type optional steering wheel that would make Tesla proud.\nVia // Mercedes-Benz Deutschland\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMercedes-Benz Mercedes-AMG Mercedes-Maybach EV sedan Europe SUV\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT US Casino Magnate’s Custom 2009 Christensen Superyacht Fetches $22M",
